{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Make a stale clone's push **refuse** instead of silently re-unioning ~1 GiB of stripped history.\nThis is the keystone that makes the reclaim safe, and it must be deployed everywhere before the\nwindow. session_log **1cb6-d553-6565-49b4** (measured facts; do not re-derive).\n\n## Why a guard is needed at all, and why in two places\nAfter the strip, `.bridge_state` still only appears from 2026-06-11, so the first **1,029 commits are\nbyte-identical** and `merge-base HEAD origin/tickets` **SUCCEEDS** (measured). So `sync.py` does NOT\ntake its unrelated-histories branch — it reaches the ordinary **diverged** path (`:100-112`) and\nunions normally. Quiescence does not close this.\n\nAnd a guard in `sync.py` alone is **bypassed**: `push.py:203` and `:229` run an INDEPENDENT\n`git merge <remote>/<branch>` on non-fast-forward rejection and then re-push (<=3 attempts), never\nconsulting `sync.py`. That is exactly the path a stale review-bot write takes: `voter.py:735` ->\n`create_ticket` -> `push_after_commit` -> `push_tickets_branch` -> rejected -> `push.py:203` merge ->\nretry **succeeds**.\n\n## Placement (precise — the wrong spot wedges every clone)\n- Inside **`sync._union_merge`** (`sync.py:114`) — the sole shared callee of both `:97` and `:111`.\n  **NOT** before the merge-base test at `:96`: that would also block the ff-adopt at `:101-104`,\n  which is exactly how a merely-behind clone converges, wedging it permanently.\n- Before **`push.py:203` and `:229`**.\n- **NOT** at `lock.acquire()` (where `check_store_compat` already fires, `lock.py:296`): a blanket\n  write block would also block the convergence operation, deadlocking the clone it rejects.\n\n## Where the epoch lives\nAdd an `epoch` field to the **existing** `.store-compat.json` rather than a parallel marker.\nVerified safe: `compat.py`'s `_analyze` validates only `format_version` and `required_capabilities`\nvia `.get()`, so **unknown keys are ignored** — an older binary neither crashes nor fails closed.\nTwo precisions: `compat.write_compat_record` (`:184`) hardcodes the two keys, so a store it stamps is\nborn epoch-absent (decide that deliberately); and `_record_path` reads only the **local worktree**, so\nthe guard needs its own `git show <remote_ref>:.store-compat.json` reader for the other side.\n\n## Expand/contract — the FULL state matrix (plan-review E2/T1 remediation)\nThe earlier wording said only \"`absent == absent` => compatible, `absent vs present` => abort\",\nwhich left two distinct absences conflated. Specify all of it. Two independent axes per side:\nthe **record** (`.store-compat.json`) may be absent or present, and within a present record the\n**`epoch` field** may be absent or present.\n\n| local | remote | behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| record absent | record absent | **compatible** (pre-feature store both sides; union proceeds) |\n| record absent | record present, epoch absent | **compatible** |\n| record present, epoch absent | record present, epoch absent | **compatible** (today's state) |\n| epoch present | epoch present, equal | **compatible** |\n| epoch present | epoch present, different | **ABORT** |\n| epoch absent | epoch present | **ABORT** (local predates the rewrite) |\n| epoch present | epoch absent | **ABORT** (remote predates local; refuse rather than guess) |\n| either side | record present but **corrupt/unparseable** | **ABORT** (never silently treat as absent — mirrors `compat.py`'s existing corrupt-record rule) |\n| remote ref unreadable (`git show` non-zero) | — | **ABORT**, distinguished in the message from \"file absent\" |\n\nNote the last two rows: `git show <remote_ref>:.store-compat.json` exits non-zero **both** when the\nfile does not exist at that ref and when the ref itself cannot be read. These must be\ndistinguished in the log message, because the remedies differ (re-clone vs fix the remote).\n\nThe epoch **value** appears only in the reclaim's re-add commit. The epoch\n**value** appears only in the reclaim's re-add commit. That way pre-window clones (both absent) merge\nnormally even holding unpushed work, while post-rewrite stale clones abort.\n\n**The guard only binds binaries at or after this change** — older ones ignore the field. So the\nreclaim window additionally requires >=24 h plus an observed `reconcile-bridge.yml` run on a\ndescendant SHA, and the review-bot convergence (A6-0b) stays in the protocol.\n\n## Message channel\n`logger.warning`, **not** an exception: every caller of `sync.reconverge` is `reads.ensure_fresh`\n(`reads.py:255-262`), which wraps it in a bare `except Exception: pass` inside a second bare\n`except Exception: return`, so a raise is swallowed with **no output**. Visibility comes from the\nentrypoint handlers (`_cli/__init__.py:408`, `mcp_server.py:688`,\n`rebar_reconciler/__main__.py:438`), not Python's `lastResort` (`__init__.py:131` attaches a\n`NullHandler`, so lastResort never fires). Known gap: `sync.push=async` spawns the push with\n`stdout/stderr=DEVNULL` (`push.py:113-145`), so those clones get no message and rely on `fsck`'s\n`PUSH_PENDING`.\n\n## Out of scope, declared\n`fsck_recover.py` cherry-picks dangling ticket commits with no epoch awareness — it creates new\ncommits rather than unioning a DAG, so blast radius is bounded. Also **not** covered: the CI bash\nunions at `reconcile-bridge.yml:321` and `reconcile-bridge-canary.yml:439`, which are protected by\nthe window protocol (workflows disabled + no in-flight run asserted), not by this guard.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A clone on a **stale epoch** attempting to push **aborts and performs no merge** at **both**\n      guarded sites — asserted separately for `sync._union_merge` and for `push.py`'s non-FF retry.\n      Tier: **integration** in two real temporary clones with divergent epochs. This is the RED case.\n- [ ] Assert on the emitted **log record** (`caplog`), plus one **subprocess** run with\n      `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always` proving the message reaches a real operator's stderr.\n      `sync.push=async` is excluded by design.\n- [ ] A merely-**behind** clone (matching epochs) still converges via ff-adopt, and a\n      **strictly-ahead** clone still returns early — neither is blocked. Tier: **integration**. This\n      is the negative control that catches the wedge-everything misplacement.\n- [ ] **Every row of the state matrix** above is asserted, including the two distinct absences\n      (record-absent vs record-present-epoch-absent) and the corrupt-record row. Tier: **unit**,\n      table-driven over the matrix — one case per row, so a missing row fails the suite.\n      Proving command: `pytest tests/unit/test_epoch_guard_matrix.py -v`.\n- [ ] `git show` failing because the **ref** is unreadable produces a different message than the file\n      being **absent** at a readable ref. Tier: **unit** with two fixtures.\n      Proving command: `pytest tests/unit/test_epoch_guard_matrix.py -k remote_read -v`.\n- [ ] An older binary reading a record that carries `epoch` does not error and does not fail closed.\n      Tier: **unit** against `compat._analyze`.\n- [ ] The remote side is read via `git show <remote_ref>:.store-compat.json`, not the local worktree.\n      Tier: **unit** with a fixture where local and remote records differ.\n\n## Files\n`src/rebar/_store/compat.py`, `src/rebar/_store/sync.py`, `src/rebar/_store/push.py`, tests\n\nScope: M. No dependencies; must land and roll out before A6-1."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785379542006305001,"uuid":"f24ffaa7-23a0-46f6-a71b-8846e0c29f1e"}